= Carbon cycle — Simple =

The '''carbon cycle''' is how carbon moves among air, oceans, soil, living things and rocks.

Plants use carbon dioxide when they make sugars through [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]. Animals and microorganisms can return carbon dioxide through respiration and decomposition. Oceans also take in and release carbon dioxide.

Some carbon stays in rocks or deep reservoirs for very long times. Burning fossil fuels moves stored carbon into the atmosphere much faster than some natural geological processes.

To understand whether carbon is building up in the air, scientists compare how much enters and leaves it.

